[
{ type: install
  message: <<EOM
Each runner needs a config file it can read and a one-time registration.
The registration is made as the user the runner runs as, from its state
directory, where it writes .runner, which holds the runner's token. For
one runner:

  %%PREFIX%%/bin/gitea-runner -c %%ETCDIR%%/config.yaml config init
  chgrp %%GITEA_RUNNER_GROUP%% %%ETCDIR%%/config.yaml
  chmod 640 %%ETCDIR%%/config.yaml
  cd /var/db/gitea-runner
  su -m %%GITEA_RUNNER_USER%% -c 'umask 027; %%PREFIX%%/bin/gitea-runner -c %%ETCDIR%%/config.yaml register'

The labels register offers by default run every job in a container
("docker://"), and the runner will not start without a Docker-compatible
API. On FreeBSD that is Podman's: install sysutils/podman-suite, give
the runner's group the socket, which otherwise belongs to group
operator, and point the runner at it:

  sysrc podman_service_enable=YES
  sysrc podman_service_api_group=%%GITEA_RUNNER_GROUP%%
  service podman_service restart
  sysrc gitea_runner_env="DOCKER_HOST=unix:///var/run/podman/podman.sock"
  %%PREFIX%%/bin/gitea-runner -c %%ETCDIR%%/config.yaml config set container.docker_host -

Runners with users of their own need that group too. Podman runs as
root, so the socket gives control of it. With container.docker_host
left empty or set to a socket, the runner also mounts that socket into
every job container, which hands that control to each workflow; "-"
keeps it out. A runner that runs jobs directly on the host needs no
Podman: give it host labels instead, such as freebsd:host, at the
prompt or with register --labels.

Then start it:

  sysrc gitea_runner_enable=YES
  newsyslog -CN /var/log/gitea-runner.log
  service syslogd reload
  service gitea_runner start

Later changes made with "config set" keep the file's owner and mode.
Output goes to /var/log/gitea-runner.log, tagged with each profile, and
also to /var/log/daemon.log through the daemon.info line in
/etc/syslog.conf.

For several, list them in gitea_runner_profiles and give each its
gitea_runner_<profile>_* variables; see
%%PREFIX%%/etc/rc.d/gitea_runner. Each profile's config is
%%ETCDIR%%/<profile>.yaml, made readable the same way,
with a group its user is in.
Each profile runs in /var/db/gitea-runner/<profile>, which keeps its
.runner: create it with install -d -o <user> -g <group> -m 750 and
register from there. The runner keeps its caches and host work
directories under its user's home, so profiles that share a user each
need their own cache.dir and host.workdir_parent in their config. That
home has to exist and be writable by the user; for a user without one,
set both in the config instead.

If you are coming from devel/gitea-act_runner: pkg upgrade does not
replace it. Delete it and register again, as above, then remove the old
runner from Gitea's Actions settings, where it stays listed as offline.
The runner now runs as %%GITEA_RUNNER_USER%%, from /var/db/gitea-runner. Its
config moves from %%PREFIX%%/etc/act_runner/act_runner.conf to
%%ETCDIR%%/config.yaml. In rc.conf, rename
act_runner_enable, _user, _group, _config and _args to gitea_runner_*.
gitea_runner_args no longer includes -c, and act_runner_facility,
_priority, _cache_dir, _log_dir, _run_dir and _log_file have no
replacement. The act_runner user is not removed.
EOM
}
]
